/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/server-plus.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("server-plus", [["sv1",0,1,"An admin is filling an empty 42U rack with a UPS, a storage array and several 1U servers. Where should the UPS go?",["At the top, near the ceiling for cooling","In the middle, at eye level for easy reading","In a separate rack beside the servers","At the bottom of the rack"],3,"Heavy items such as UPS units go at the bottom to keep the rack's center of gravity low. Mounting them high makes the rack top-heavy and risky when servers slide out.","Objective 1.1",["Mounting a heavy UPS high makes the rack top-heavy and prone to tipping; cooling is not improved by height.","Eye-level placement is a convenience, not a safety rule, and it still raises the rack's center of gravity.","A UPS belongs at the bottom of the same rack; putting it in a separate rack wastes space and is unnecessary.",null],1],["sv2",0,1,"A data center uses a hot aisle/cold aisle layout. Which way should the servers be installed?",["Fronts facing the hot aisle so exhaust is cooled","Fronts facing the cold aisle so intake draws cool air","Alternating directions in each rack","Sideways so air flows freely between neighbouring racks and rows"],1,"Servers draw cool air in through the front and exhaust from the back, so fronts face the cold aisle. Facing the hot aisle would pull in exhaust air.","Objective 1.1",["Facing fronts into the hot aisle makes intakes pull in warm exhaust air, causing the servers to overheat.",null,"Alternating rack directions mixes hot exhaust with cold intake, defeating the hot/cold aisle separation.","Mounting servers sideways breaks the aisle airflow design and lets exhaust recirculate into intakes."],1],["sv3",0,1,"A server has two power supplies. To remove single points of failure in the power path, how should they be connected?",["Each into a separate PDU fed by a separate circuit","Both into the same PDU on one UPS","One into a PDU and one into a wall outlet","Both into a surge-protected power strip plugged into a single UPS"],0,"Separate PDUs on separate circuits (A and B feeds) mean one PDU, UPS or breaker failure does not take the server down.","Objective 1.1",[null,"One PDU on a single UPS is itself a single point of failure, so a fault there still drops the server.","A wall outlet is unconditioned and unprotected, and mixing it with a PDU leaves an unbalanced power path.","A power strip and a single UPS are both single points of failure feeding both supplies at once."],2],["sv4",0,1,"Six 4 TB drives are configured as RAID 6. What is the usable capacity?",["24 TB","20 TB","16 TB","12 TB"],2,"RAID 6 uses two drives' worth of capacity for dual parity: (6 − 2) × 4 TB = 16 TB. RAID 5 would give 20 TB.","Objective 1.2",["24 TB is the raw total (or RAID 0) and ignores the capacity that dual parity consumes.","20 TB is the RAID 5 result, which reserves only one drive of parity, not the two RAID 6 uses.",null,"12 TB subtracts three drives' worth of capacity; RAID 6 only reserves two for its dual parity."],2],["sv5",0,1,"A database server needs the best write performance and must survive a drive failure. Rebuild time should be short. Which RAID level fits?",["RAID 10","RAID 5","RAID 0","RAID 6 with a hot spare"],0,"RAID 10 stripes across mirrors, so writes have no parity penalty and rebuilds just copy a mirror. RAID 5 and 6 add parity write penalties and long rebuilds.","Objective 1.2",[null,"RAID 5 adds a parity write penalty and rebuilds slowly by recalculating parity across all drives.","RAID 0 has no redundancy at all, so a single drive failure destroys the entire array.","RAID 6's dual parity slows writes further and its rebuilds are long, even with a hot spare on hand."],3],["sv6",0,1,"Which storage option presents block-level LUNs to servers over a standard Ethernet network?",["NAS using SMB","iSCSI SAN","DAS over SAS","NFS export"],1,"iSCSI carries SCSI block commands over TCP/IP, so servers see LUNs as local disks. NAS protocols such as SMB and NFS share files, not blocks.","Objective 1.2",["NAS with SMB shares data at the file level over the network, not as raw block-level LUNs.",null,"DAS over SAS is block storage but is attached directly to one server, not delivered over Ethernet.","NFS is a file-level network share, so servers see mounted folders rather than block LUNs."],1],["sv7",0,1,"An admin must replace a failed drive in a production server without shutting it down. Which feature must the drive bay and controller support?",["Write-back cache","Secure Boot","Hot-swap","Wake-on-LAN"],2,"Hot-swappable bays let a drive be removed and replaced while the system runs. Write-back cache improves performance but does not allow live replacement.","Objective 1.2",["Write-back cache boosts write performance but does nothing to allow replacing a drive while powered on.","Secure Boot verifies signed boot components and is unrelated to live drive replacement.",null,"Wake-on-LAN powers a system on remotely and has nothing to do with hot-swapping a drive."],1],["sv8",0,1,"A new 8 TB drive shows as about 7.28 TB in the operating system. What explains the difference?",["Space reserved for the RAID controller","A failing drive with bad sectors","The file system journal","Base-10 vs base-2 capacity calculation"],3,"Vendors count 1 TB as 10^12 bytes; the OS reports in 2^40-byte units. 8 × 10^12 / 2^40 ≈ 7.28.","Objective 1.2",["No RAID is mentioned, and controllers do not reserve enough space to explain the shortfall.","The reduced figure is normal reporting math, not a symptom of a failing drive with bad sectors.","A file system journal is tiny and cannot account for hundreds of gigabytes of apparent difference.",null],2],["sv9",0,1,"A remote server has hung and the OS is unreachable over the network. Which tool lets the admin power-cycle it and watch POST remotely?",["RDP","The BMC, such as iDRAC or iLO","SSH","SNMP traps sent to the monitoring console"],1,"A baseboard management controller runs independently of the OS on its own network port, providing remote power control and console. RDP and SSH need a working OS.","Objective 1.3",["RDP relies on a running OS and network stack, so it cannot help when the OS is unreachable.",null,"SSH also needs a working OS and service, which is exactly what has failed here.","SNMP traps only push alert messages; they cannot power-cycle hardware or display the POST screen."],1],["sv10",0,1,"Which setting should be enabled in UEFI to ensure only signed bootloaders and OS kernels load?",["Secure Boot","Legacy boot","PXE boot","Fast boot"],0,"Secure Boot checks signatures on boot components against trusted keys. Legacy (CSM) boot disables that check.","Objective 1.3",[null,"Legacy (CSM) boot disables signature checking, which is the opposite of what is wanted.","PXE boot loads an OS or installer over the network and does not enforce signed bootloaders.","Fast boot skips parts of startup for speed and does not validate boot component signatures."],1],["sv11",0,1,"An admin needs to connect two top-of-rack switches with a short, low-cost 10 Gbps link. Which option fits best?",["Single-mode fiber with LR optics","A long Cat5e patch cable run through the overhead tray","Twinax direct attach copper (DAC) cable","Coaxial RG-6 cable"],2,"A passive DAC is inexpensive and ideal for short 10 Gbps runs between SFP+ ports. LR optics work but cost far more for a few meters.","Objective 1.1",["Single-mode fiber with LR optics works but costs far more than needed for a few-meter link.","Cat5e is not rated for reliable 10 Gbps and is a poor fit for a short, high-speed switch interconnect.",null,"RG-6 coax is for video/CATV signaling and cannot carry a 10 Gbps SFP+ switch link."],2],["sv12",0,1,"Before updating a server's firmware, what should the admin do first?",["Disable the host firewall","Reset the BIOS to defaults","Remove the redundant power supplies to avoid a surge during flashing","Check release notes and compatibility, and have a rollback plan"],3,"Firmware updates can break drivers or hardware support, so review the notes, test and plan rollback in a maintenance window. Resetting BIOS defaults may undo needed settings.","Objective 1.3",["Disabling the host firewall has no bearing on flashing firmware and only weakens security.","Resetting the BIOS to defaults would wipe needed settings rather than prepare for the update.","Removing power supplies risks losing power mid-flash, which can brick the firmware.",null],2],["sv13",0,1,"A server needs memory that can detect and correct single-bit errors. What should be ordered?",["Non-parity desktop DIMMs","ECC memory","Faster-clocked unbuffered DIMMs","Extra swap space on SSD"],1,"Error-correcting code memory detects and fixes single-bit errors, which is standard for servers. Faster non-ECC memory offers no correction.","Objective 1.3",["Non-parity desktop DIMMs cannot detect or correct any memory errors.",null,"Faster unbuffered non-ECC memory offers speed but no error correction, which servers need.","Adding SSD swap is disk paging and does nothing to correct in-memory bit errors."],1],["sv14",0,1,"A small office has no rack space and needs one quiet, standalone server under a desk. Which form factor fits?",["Blade","2U rack mount","Tower","1U rack mount"],2,"Tower servers stand alone and are quieter, suiting offices without racks. Blades need an enclosure and rack servers need rails and a rack.","Objective 1.1",["A blade server requires an enclosure and a rack, which this office does not have.","A 2U rack-mount server still needs a rack and rail kit to install.",null,"A 1U rack server needs a rack and its small high-speed fans are loud for an office."],1],["sv15",0,1,"An admin needs to know how long a UPS will run the rack during an outage. Which two values matter most?",["Battery capacity and the total load in watts","Rack height and number of servers","Input voltage, outlet type and the rack's physical height","Server CPU speed and RAM"],0,"Runtime depends on how much energy the batteries hold and how much power the load draws. Outlet types matter for connection, not runtime.","Objective 1.1",[null,"Rack height and server count describe physical size, not how long the batteries can sustain the load.","Voltage and outlet type matter for connection, and physical height is irrelevant to runtime energy math.","CPU speed and RAM do not directly give the wattage draw needed to calculate runtime."],2],["sv16",0,1,"Which drive interface offers the lowest latency by connecting flash storage directly over PCIe lanes?",["SATA","SAS","USB 3","NVMe"],3,"NVMe was designed for flash and uses PCIe directly, avoiding the SATA/SAS controller layers. SAS is enterprise-grade but still slower than NVMe.","Objective 1.2",["SATA is an older AHCI interface with higher latency than a direct PCIe flash connection.","SAS is enterprise-grade but still routes through a controller layer, making it slower than NVMe.","USB 3 is an external peripheral bus, not a low-latency internal storage path.",null],1],["sv17",0,2,"An admin must install the same OS image on 40 new servers over the network without media. Which method fits?",["Install from USB on each one","PXE boot to a deployment server","Clone with a Type 2 hypervisor","Use a P2V conversion from a reference server"],1,"PXE lets servers boot from the network and pull an image or unattended install from a deployment server. USB installs do not scale to 40 servers.","Objective 2.1",["Installing from USB on each of 40 servers is manual and does not scale.",null,"A Type 2 hypervisor runs on a desktop OS and is not a tool for mass bare-metal deployment.","P2V converts an existing physical server to a VM; it does not deploy an OS to 40 new machines."],2],["sv18",0,2,"Before buying new servers for an OS, what should the admin check to confirm the hardware is supported?",["The SLA","The OS end-user license agreement terms","The hardware compatibility list","The asset inventory"],2,"The HCL lists hardware the OS vendor has tested and supports. The license covers usage rights, not hardware support.","Objective 2.1",["An SLA defines service commitments and says nothing about which hardware the OS supports.","The EULA covers usage rights, not whether the hardware has been tested by the OS vendor.",null,"The asset inventory lists owned equipment, not vendor-tested compatibility for a new OS."],1],["sv19",0,2,"A company wants to reduce the attack surface and resource use of Windows file servers managed remotely. Which installation option fits?",["Server Core","Desktop Experience","A Type 2 hypervisor","A 32-bit edition"],0,"Server Core has no full GUI, so it uses fewer resources and has fewer components to patch. Desktop Experience adds the GUI and its attack surface.","Objective 2.1",[null,"Desktop Experience adds the full GUI, increasing both resource use and attack surface.","A Type 2 hypervisor is a virtualization product, not a Windows Server installation option.","A 32-bit edition limits memory and is not how you reduce attack surface; Server is 64-bit anyway."],2],["sv20",0,2,"A physical legacy server must be moved onto a hypervisor with minimal rebuild work. What process is used?",["V2P","PXE install","Bare-metal restore","P2V"],3,"Physical-to-virtual conversion copies the running server's disks and configuration into a VM. V2P is the reverse direction.","Objective 2.1",["V2P is the reverse process, converting a virtual machine back to physical hardware.","A PXE install builds a fresh OS over the network, which would mean rebuilding, not migrating.","A bare-metal restore recovers a backup onto hardware; it does not convert the server into a VM.",null],1],["sv21",0,2,"A Linux file server needs a file system that handles very large files and volumes well and is the default on RHEL. Which one fits?",["FAT32","exFAT","XFS","NTFS"],2,"XFS is RHEL's default file system and scales to large files and volumes. FAT32 limits files to 4 GB.","Objective 2.1",["FAT32 limits single files to 4 GB and is unsuitable for large server volumes.","exFAT targets removable flash media, not large enterprise Linux file servers.",null,"NTFS is the Windows file system and is not the default on RHEL."],1],["sv22",0,2,"A server's two NICs are combined so traffic continues if one link or switch port fails. What is this called?",["VLAN tagging","NIC teaming","Port mirroring","Jumbo frames"],1,"NIC teaming (bonding) groups adapters for failover and often load balancing. Port mirroring copies traffic for analysis.","Objective 2.2",["VLAN tagging segments traffic into virtual LANs; it does not provide link failover.",null,"Port mirroring copies traffic to a monitoring port and offers no redundancy.","Jumbo frames raise the MTU for throughput and do nothing for link failover."],1],["sv23",0,2,"A new domain controller keeps reporting Kerberos authentication failures, and its clock is 12 minutes off. Which service should be configured?",["DHCP","SNMP","SMTP","NTP"],3,"Kerberos tolerates only small clock skew (5 minutes by default), so servers must sync time with NTP. SNMP is for monitoring.","Objective 2.2",["DHCP assigns IP addresses and has no role in synchronizing the server's clock.","SNMP is for monitoring devices, not for keeping time in sync for Kerberos.","SMTP transports email and is unrelated to clock skew or authentication.",null],3],["sv24",0,2,"A printer server needs the same IP address every time, but the admin wants to manage it from the DHCP server. What should be created?",["A DHCP reservation","A DHCP exclusion","A new scope","A DNS CNAME record"],0,"A reservation ties an IP address to the device's MAC so it always gets the same lease. An exclusion just removes addresses from the pool.","Objective 2.2",[null,"An exclusion removes addresses from the pool but does not assign a fixed address to a device.","A new scope defines a range of addresses, not a guaranteed fixed lease for one device.","A CNAME is a DNS alias and does not control which IP address the printer receives."],2],["sv25",0,2,"A hypervisor host has one physical NIC connected to a trunk port. VMs on it must sit in different VLANs. What must be configured?",["VLAN IDs on the virtual switch port groups or VM adapters","A separate physical switch and uplink cable for every VM","NIC teaming on each guest","A DHCP relay on the host"],0,"Tagging traffic with VLAN IDs on the virtual switch lets one trunked uplink carry several VLANs. Separate switches per VM are unnecessary.","Objective 2.2",[null,"A separate physical switch per VM is unnecessary because a trunked uplink already carries all VLANs.","NIC teaming on the guest aggregates links for redundancy, not VLAN separation.","A DHCP relay forwards DHCP requests across subnets; it does not place VMs into VLANs."],3],["sv26",0,2,"Two web servers sit behind a load balancer. One often has many long-running sessions while the other is idle. Which algorithm balances this better than round robin?",["Source IP hash","Least connections","Static weighting by server age","DNS round robin"],1,"Least connections sends new requests to the server with the fewest active sessions, adapting to long-lived connections. Round robin ignores current load.","Objective 2.4",["Source IP hash pins each client to a server by address and does not even out uneven session lengths.",null,"Weighting by server age is arbitrary and ignores how busy each server actually is.","DNS round robin just rotates returned records with no awareness of current server load."],2],["sv27",0,2,"A two-node cluster lost its network link between nodes, and both tried to own the shared disk. What prevents this?",["Round-robin DNS","A heartbeat alone","A quorum with a witness","NIC teaming on the storage network"],2,"A witness gives one side the majority vote, so only one partition keeps running and split-brain is avoided. A heartbeat detects failure but cannot break the tie.","Objective 2.4",["Round-robin DNS distributes name lookups and does nothing to prevent split-brain.","A heartbeat alone can detect a node failure but cannot break the tie over shared-disk ownership.",null,"NIC teaming on the storage network adds link redundancy but does not arbitrate cluster ownership."],3],["sv28",0,2,"A standby node sits idle until the primary database server fails, then takes over. What configuration is this?",["Active-active","Scale-out clustering","Load balanced","Active-passive"],3,"In active-passive clustering the passive node waits to take over on failure. Active-active runs all nodes at once.","Objective 2.4",["In active-active clustering all nodes serve traffic at once, not one sitting idle.","Scale-out clustering adds nodes to grow capacity, not a passive standby waiting to take over.","Load balancing spreads work across active nodes rather than keeping one on standby.",null],1],["sv29",0,2,"A server connects to a SAN over two HBAs and two fabrics. What software provides failover and load sharing across both paths?",["RAID","NIC teaming","MPIO","iSCSI CHAP"],2,"Multipath I/O manages several paths to the same LUN, failing over and balancing between them. CHAP only authenticates iSCSI sessions.","Objective 2.4",["RAID provides disk redundancy within an array, not failover across SAN fabric paths.","NIC teaming aggregates Ethernet adapters and does not manage storage HBA paths.",null,"iSCSI CHAP only authenticates iSCSI sessions and provides no path failover or load sharing."],2],["sv30",0,2,"A company wants to run production VMs directly on server hardware without a host OS. Which hypervisor type fits?",["Emulator","Type 2","Container runtime","Type 1"],3,"Type 1 (bare-metal) hypervisors such as ESXi, Hyper-V and KVM run directly on hardware. Type 2 runs on top of a desktop OS.","Objective 2.5",["An emulator simulates hardware and is slow, not a bare-metal production hypervisor.","A Type 2 hypervisor runs on top of a host desktop OS, not directly on the hardware.","A container runtime shares the host kernel and is not a hardware-level hypervisor.",null],1],["sv31",0,2,"A host has 64 GB of RAM, but its VMs are assigned 96 GB in total. What is this practice called?",["Memory ballooning failure","Memory overcommitment","Thin provisioning","NUMA pinning"],1,"Assigning more virtual resources than physically exist is overcommitment; it works while VMs do not use their full allocation at once. Thin provisioning refers to storage.","Objective 2.5",["Ballooning is a technique to reclaim guest memory, not the term for assigning more than exists.",null,"Thin provisioning refers to allocating storage on demand, not to RAM allocation.","NUMA pinning binds a VM to specific CPU/memory nodes and is unrelated to overallocation."],1],["sv32",0,2,"An admin wants a quick rollback point before patching a VM. What should they take?",["A snapshot","A clone to a new host","A full backup to tape","A template"],0,"A snapshot captures the VM's state for a fast revert and should be deleted after the change. Templates are for deploying new VMs.","Objective 2.5",[null,"Cloning makes a full copy to another host, which is slower than a snapshot for a quick rollback.","A full backup to tape is slow and meant for disaster recovery, not a fast pre-patch revert.","A template is a master image for deploying new VMs, not a rollback point for this one."],1],["sv33",0,2,"A VM's virtual disk is configured to use physical space only as data is written. What is this?",["Thick provisioning","Thin provisioning","Deduplication","Pass-through disk"],1,"Thin-provisioned disks grow on demand, saving space but risking the datastore filling up. Thick provisioning allocates everything at creation.","Objective 2.5",["Thick provisioning allocates all the disk's space up front, the opposite of growing on demand.",null,"Deduplication removes duplicate data blocks and is a different storage-efficiency feature.","A pass-through disk maps a physical disk directly to the VM and is not about on-demand growth."],1],["sv34",0,2,"A company rents VMs from a cloud provider and manages the OS, patches and apps itself. Which service model is this?",["SaaS","PaaS","FaaS","IaaS"],3,"In IaaS the provider runs hardware and virtualization while the customer manages the OS upward. In PaaS the provider also manages the OS.","Objective 2.5",["In SaaS the provider manages the entire application, so the customer would not patch the OS.","In PaaS the provider manages the OS and runtime, leaving the customer only apps and data.","FaaS is serverless functions where the customer never manages an OS at all.",null],2],["sv35",0,2,"In a PowerShell script, which comparison operator tests whether $disk.FreeGB is less than 10?",["$disk.FreeGB -less 10","$disk.FreeGB < 10","$disk.FreeGB -lt 10","$disk.FreeGB -le 1"],2,"PowerShell uses -lt for less than; the < symbol is a redirection operator in PowerShell, not a comparison.","Objective 2.6",["-less is not a valid PowerShell comparison operator.","In PowerShell the < symbol is a redirection operator, not a less-than comparison.",null,"-le is less-than-or-equal, and this option compares to 1 rather than to 10."],2],["sv36",0,2,"An admin needs a Bash script to run every night at 2:00 AM on a Linux server. What should be used?",["A cron job","A systemd mount unit","An .bashrc entry","The at command once"],0,"cron runs scheduled jobs repeatedly (for example `0 2 * * *`). The at command runs a job only once.","Objective 2.6",[null,"A systemd mount unit mounts filesystems and is not a job scheduler.","An .bashrc entry runs at interactive shell login, not on a nightly schedule.","The at command schedules a job to run only once, not every night."],1],["sv37",0,2,"A script must create 200 user accounts listed in a CSV file. Which scripting construct is most useful?",["A comparator","A loop","A single variable","An exit code"],1,"A loop (such as foreach) repeats the account creation for each row. Comparators only test values.","Objective 2.6",["A comparator only tests values and cannot repeat the account-creation task for each row.",null,"A single variable holds one value and cannot iterate over 200 CSV rows.","An exit code reports whether the script succeeded, not a way to repeat an action."],1],["sv38",0,2,"What is the main risk of storing an administrator password in plain text inside a script?",["The script will run slower","The script cannot be scheduled","Anyone who reads the script gains the credential","It prevents the script's variables from being passed to functions"],2,"Plain-text credentials can be read by anyone with access to the file or its backups. Use a secrets vault, managed service accounts or encrypted credentials instead.","Objective 2.6",["Storing a password in plain text does not affect how fast the script runs.","A plain-text credential does not stop the script from being scheduled.",null,"Passing a variable to a function still works; plain-text storage does not prevent that."],2],["sv39",0,2,"A database product is licensed per core. A company replaces a 16-core server with one that has 64 cores. What happens to licensing cost?",["It increases because more cores must be licensed","It stays the same since it is one server","It decreases because newer CPUs complete the same work in less time","It changes only if users increase"],0,"Per-core licensing scales with core count, so four times the cores means more licenses. Per-server or per-user models would not change.","Objective 2.8",[null,"Per-core licensing is not per-server, so quadrupling the cores raises the license count.","Faster completion does not reduce the number of cores that must be licensed.","Per-core cost depends on cores, not on the number of users."],2],["sv40",0,2,"Each of 300 employees accesses a Windows file server from several devices. Which Client Access License model is usually more economical?",["Per-device CALs","Site license","Per-socket licenses","Per-user CALs"],3,"Per-user CALs cover a person on any number of devices, so they are cheaper when users have several devices. Per-device suits shared devices.","Objective 2.8",["Per-device CALs cost more when each user connects from several devices.","A site license is broad and usually far more expensive than needed for 300 users.","Per-socket licensing is based on server hardware and is unrelated to user counts.",null],3],["sv41",0,2,"An auditor asks when each server's hardware warranty expires. Which document should hold this?",["The asset inventory","The network diagram","The change log","The service's runbook"],0,"Asset records track serials, purchase dates, warranty and life-cycle status. Network diagrams show connections.","Objective 2.7",[null,"A network diagram shows how devices connect, not hardware warranty expiration dates.","A change log records modifications made to systems, not warranty information.","A runbook documents operating procedures, not asset warranty details."],2],["sv42",0,2,"A team documents normal CPU, memory and disk use for a new server during its first month. What is this record called?",["An SLA","A baseline","A service catalog","A root cause analysis"],1,"A performance baseline records normal behaviour so later anomalies stand out. An SLA defines service commitments.","Objective 2.7",["An SLA defines service commitments and targets, not the measured normal resource usage.",null,"A service catalog lists the services offered to users, not performance measurements.","A root cause analysis explains an incident afterward, not routine baseline behavior."],1],["sv43",0,2,"Before changing a production server's RAID configuration, what should normally be approved?",["A decommissioning ticket for the old array configuration","A new SLA","A license renewal","A change request through change management"],3,"Change management reviews risk, schedules a window and documents a backout plan before production changes. An SLA is unrelated to one change.","Objective 2.7",["A decommissioning ticket is for retiring assets, not for modifying a running array's config.","A new SLA sets service commitments and is not the approval needed for one config change.","A license renewal covers software entitlements and is unrelated to a RAID change.",null],2],["sv44",0,3,"A backup job runs a full on Sunday and incrementals Monday to Friday. The server fails Thursday afternoon. Which backups are needed to restore?",["Sunday's full and Wednesday's incremental, which includes all earlier changes","Only Thursday's incremental","Sunday's full and every incremental from Monday to Thursday","Sunday's full only"],2,"Each incremental holds only changes since the previous backup, so all of them are needed after the full. With differentials, only the latest would be needed.","Objective 3.7",["Sunday plus only Wednesday's incremental skips Thursday's changes and the other incrementals.","Thursday's incremental alone lacks the base full and all the intervening changes.",null,"The full alone would lose every change made during the week."],3],["sv45",0,3,"Restores must be as fast as possible using only two backup sets, and nightly backup time can grow during the week. Which scheme fits?",["Weekly full with daily differentials","Weekly full with daily incrementals","Monthly full only","Daily snapshots kept on the same volume"],0,"A differential holds all changes since the last full, so a restore needs just the full and the latest differential. Incrementals need every set in the chain.","Objective 3.7",[null,"Incrementals require restoring the full plus every set in the chain, more than two backup sets.","A monthly full alone leaves a huge data-loss window and no fast recent restore.","Daily snapshots on the same volume are not independent and are lost if that volume fails."],3],["sv46",0,3,"Which practice best follows the 3-2-1 backup rule?",["Three backups a day, two of them full, one of them tested","Three copies, on two media types, with one copy off-site","Three servers, two sites, one cloud","Three snapshots on two volumes of one array"],1,"3-2-1 means three copies of data on two kinds of media with one copy stored off-site. Snapshots on one array fail together.","Objective 3.7",["This describes backup frequency and testing, not the copies/media/off-site structure of 3-2-1.",null,"Counting servers and sites conflates infrastructure with the required data copies and media types.","Snapshots on one array all fail together and meet neither the two-media nor the off-site rule."],1],["sv47",0,3,"A company's backups run every night, but nobody has ever restored from them. What is the biggest risk?",["Backups take too long","Backup media will be full","The backups may be unusable when needed","Backup software licensing costs will rise every year"],2,"Untested backups may be corrupt, incomplete or missing data, which is only discovered during a disaster. Regular test restores prove recoverability.","Objective 3.7",["Long backup times are a performance concern, not the core danger of never testing restores.","Full media is a capacity issue and does not address whether the data can actually be recovered.",null,"Rising licensing costs are a budget matter, unrelated to whether the backups can restore."],2],["sv48",0,3,"A business can lose no more than 15 minutes of order data after an outage. Which metric does this define?",["RTO","MTTR","MTBF","RPO"],3,"RPO is the maximum tolerable data loss measured in time. RTO is how quickly service must be restored.","Objective 3.8",["RTO measures how quickly service must be restored, not how much data can be lost.","MTTR is the mean time to repair a failure, a reliability metric, not a data-loss target.","MTBF is the mean time between failures and does not describe tolerable data loss.",null],2],["sv49",0,3,"The payroll system must be running again within four hours of a disaster. Which metric does this define?",["RPO","MTBF","RTO","SLA credit"],2,"RTO is the maximum time allowed to restore a service. RPO measures acceptable data loss.","Objective 3.8",["RPO is the acceptable amount of lost data in time, not how fast service must return.","MTBF describes expected time between failures, not a recovery deadline.",null,"An SLA credit is compensation for a missed service level, not a recovery-time metric."],2],["sv50",0,3,"A company needs a DR site that can take over within minutes with current data. Cost is a secondary concern. Which site type fits?",["Cold site","Warm site","Reciprocal agreement","Hot site"],3,"A hot site has running equipment and up-to-date data for near-immediate failover. A warm site needs data restored first.","Objective 3.8",["A cold site has only space and power, taking days to weeks to bring online.","A warm site has hardware but needs data restored first, so it cannot fail over in minutes.","A reciprocal agreement relies on another organization's site with uncertain readiness.",null],2],["sv51",0,3,"Data is replicated to a DR site 800 km away. Writes must not wait for the remote site to confirm. Which replication type fits?",["Synchronous","Asynchronous","Snapshot-only","Mirrored RAID 1"],1,"Asynchronous replication acknowledges writes locally and sends them later, avoiding long-distance latency at the cost of some possible data loss. Synchronous waits for both sites.","Objective 3.8",["Synchronous replication waits for the remote site to confirm each write, adding heavy latency over 800 km.",null,"Snapshot-only replication captures periodic points in time rather than continuously sending writes.","Mirrored RAID 1 duplicates disks locally and does not replicate to a distant DR site."],3],["sv52",0,3,"Managers meet to talk through each step of the DR plan for a simulated flood without touching any systems. What kind of test is this?",["Tabletop exercise","Live failover test","Parallel test","Full interruption test"],0,"A tabletop exercise walks through the plan in discussion only. Live failover actually moves production to the DR site.","Objective 3.8",[null,"A live failover test actually shifts production to the DR site, which this exercise avoids.","A parallel test runs DR systems alongside production using real infrastructure, not just discussion.","A full interruption test shuts down production to prove DR, the most disruptive kind."],2],["sv53",0,3,"Old SSDs from retired servers must be made unrecoverable before disposal. Which method is reliable?",["Degaussing","Quick format","Deleting the partitions","Physical shredding"],3,"Shredding destroys the flash chips. Degaussing does not affect SSDs, and formatting or deleting partitions leaves data recoverable.","Objective 3.6",["Degaussing erases magnetic media and has no effect on an SSD's flash memory.","A quick format only clears the file table and leaves the underlying data recoverable.","Deleting partitions removes the partition table but leaves the actual data intact.",null],3],["sv54",0,3,"After a vendor destroys a batch of old hard drives, what should the company keep for its records?",["A certificate of destruction","The drives' warranty cards","A new baseline","The original purchase order and shipping receipts"],0,"A certificate of destruction documents which assets were destroyed, when and how, supporting audits and compliance.","Objective 3.6",[null,"Warranty cards concern support entitlements, not proof that the drives were destroyed.","A new baseline records performance behavior and is unrelated to media disposal.","Purchase orders and shipping receipts document acquisition, not secure destruction."],1],["sv55",0,3,"A new web server has FTP, Telnet and a print spooler running but only needs HTTPS. What is the first hardening step?",["Install a second NIC","Disable or remove unneeded services","Increase the RAID level","Enable jumbo frames on the server's network interface"],1,"Removing services that are not needed closes ports and reduces attack surface. Telnet also sends credentials in clear text.","Objective 3.5",["Adding a second NIC provides network redundancy, not a reduction in attack surface.",null,"Raising the RAID level improves storage resilience but does not harden the OS.","Enabling jumbo frames tunes network throughput and is not a security hardening step."],2],["sv56",0,3,"Admins connect to Linux servers with passwords over SSH, and logs show constant brute-force attempts. Which change most improves security?",["Move SSH to Telnet","Allow direct root login so fixes can be made faster","Use key-based authentication and disable password login","Turn off the host firewall"],2,"Key-based SSH defeats password guessing entirely. Telnet is unencrypted, and allowing root login increases risk.","Objective 3.5",["Telnet is unencrypted and far less secure than SSH, so moving to it worsens security.","Allowing direct root login increases the risk that a compromise gains full control.",null,"Turning off the host firewall exposes more services and weakens security."],3],["sv57",0,3,"A help desk group needs to reset passwords but must not manage servers. Which principle guides this?",["Separation of duties","Defense in depth","Least privilege","Implicit trust"],2,"Least privilege grants only the rights needed for a job. Separation of duties splits critical tasks among people, which is related but different.","Objective 3.3",["Separation of duties splits critical tasks among people; it is related but not about minimal rights.","Defense in depth layers multiple controls and is not specifically about limiting account rights.",null,"Implicit trust is the opposite of secure design and grants access without verification."],2],["sv58",0,3,"Accounts are assigned to groups like 'DB-Admins' and permissions are granted to the groups. What model is this?",["Discretionary access control","Role-based access control","Mandatory access control","Rule-based access control"],1,"RBAC grants rights to roles or groups, and users inherit them through membership. MAC uses labels and clearances.","Objective 3.3",["Discretionary access control lets object owners set permissions individually, not via role groups.",null,"Mandatory access control uses security labels and clearances enforced by the system.","Rule-based access control applies conditions like time or ACLs, not role membership."],1],["sv59",0,3,"Which physical control prevents one person from following another through a secured data center door?",["An access control vestibule (mantrap)","Badge reader alone","CCTV camera","Locked rack doors"],0,"A vestibule lets one person through at a time between two doors, stopping tailgating. A camera only records it.","Objective 3.2",[null,"A badge reader alone does not stop a second person from slipping through behind an authorized one.","A CCTV camera records tailgating after the fact but does not physically prevent it.","Locked rack doors protect the equipment racks, not the data center entrance."],2],["sv60",0,3,"A laptop holding a copy of a server database was stolen. What control would most likely have protected the data?",["NIC teaming","A cable lock on the server","A longer DHCP lease","Full-disk encryption"],3,"Full-disk encryption keeps data at rest unreadable without the key. The stolen device, not the server, held the data.","Objective 3.1",["NIC teaming provides network redundancy and does nothing to protect stored data.","A cable lock secures the server, but here it was the laptop that was stolen.","A longer DHCP lease only affects addressing and offers no data protection.",null],2],["sv61",0,3,"An admin wants to stop anyone at the console from changing boot order to start a live USB OS. What should be set?",["A longer screen saver timeout","A BIOS/UEFI administrator password","A DNS PTR record","Port security on the switch port the server uses"],1,"A firmware password blocks unauthorised changes to boot settings. Screen savers do not protect firmware.","Objective 3.1",["A screen saver timeout is an OS-level lock and does not stop firmware or boot-order changes.",null,"A DNS PTR record maps an IP to a name and is unrelated to firmware security.","Switch port security controls MAC addresses on the network, not the server's boot settings."],2],["sv62",0,3,"Which tool can detect and block sensitive files, such as customer records, from being copied to USB drives or emailed out?",["MPIO","IDS","NTP","DLP"],3,"Data loss prevention inspects data in use and in motion and blocks transfers that match policy. An IDS detects network attacks.","Objective 3.4",["MPIO manages redundant storage paths and has nothing to do with blocking data exfiltration.","An IDS detects network intrusions but does not enforce policy on files copied to USB or email.","NTP synchronizes clocks and provides no data-loss protection.",null],2],["sv63",0,3,"Ransomware encrypted a file server and also the backups stored on a network share it could reach. Which change best protects future backups?",["Keep an offline or immutable copy of backups","Back up more often to the same share","Switch to differential backups","Use a larger RAID 6 array with more parity drives"],0,"Offline, air-gapped or immutable backups cannot be altered by ransomware on the network. Backing up more often to the same share leaves them exposed.","Objective 3.4",[null,"Backing up more often to the same reachable share still leaves those copies open to encryption.","Choosing differential backups changes the backup type but not the ransomware's ability to reach them.","A larger RAID 6 array protects against disk failure, not against ransomware encrypting the files."],3],["sv64",0,3,"A backup rotation keeps daily, weekly and monthly sets, reusing the daily media each week. What scheme is this?",["Tower of Hanoi","3-2-1","Grandfather-father-son","First in, first out"],2,"GFS keeps sons (daily), fathers (weekly) and grandfathers (monthly) for longer retention. 3-2-1 is a copy-placement rule, not a rotation.","Objective 3.7",["Tower of Hanoi is a different rotation based on recursive reuse intervals, not daily/weekly/monthly sets.","3-2-1 is a copy-placement rule about media and off-site copies, not a retention rotation.",null,"FIFO simply overwrites the oldest media and lacks the tiered daily/weekly/monthly retention."],2],["sv65",0,3,"Company policy says financial records must be kept for seven years and then deleted. What is this policy called?",["Data classification","Data masking","Data sovereignty","Data retention"],3,"A retention policy states how long data is kept and when it is disposed of. Data sovereignty concerns which country's laws apply to where data is stored.","Objective 3.1",["Data classification labels how sensitive data is, not how long it must be kept.","Data masking obscures values to protect them and is unrelated to retention periods.","Data sovereignty concerns which country's laws govern where data is stored.",null],1],["sv66",0,4,"A technician has identified the problem with a server. What is the next step in the CompTIA troubleshooting methodology?",["Establish a theory of probable cause","Implement the solution","Document the findings","Verify full system functionality"],0,"After identifying the problem, the technician forms a theory of probable cause and then tests it. Implementing comes later.","Objective 4.1",[null,"Implementing the solution comes only after a plan of action, much later in the process.","Documenting findings is the final step, not what follows problem identification.","Verifying functionality happens near the end, after the solution is implemented."],1],["sv67",0,4,"Before making changes to fix a failing server, the technician should do what as part of identifying the problem?",["Replace the motherboard","Perform a backup if possible","Document lessons learned","Escalate immediately"],1,"The methodology says to back up where possible before changes so data is safe. Documenting comes last.","Objective 4.1",["Replacing the motherboard is a fix action, not part of identifying the problem.",null,"Documenting lessons learned is the last step of the methodology, not an early one.","Escalating immediately skips the investigation that identification requires."],2],["sv68",0,4,"A technician fixed a failed service and confirmed users can work. What should happen last?",["Question the users again","Establish a new theory","Document findings, actions and outcomes","Reboot the server"],2,"Documentation is the final step, so the fix is recorded for future incidents. Verifying functionality comes just before it.","Objective 4.1",["Questioning users is part of identifying the problem, not the closing step.","Establishing a theory happens early, well before the fix is verified.",null,"Rebooting is a possible action, not the final documentation step."],1],["sv69",0,4,"A server beeps at power-on and does not reach the OS. Which is the most likely cause to check first?",["A DNS misconfiguration","An expired certificate","A POST hardware failure such as memory","A full data volume"],2,"Beep codes are POST messages about hardware, commonly memory, CPU or video. DNS and certificates matter only after the OS loads.","Objective 4.2",["A DNS misconfiguration only matters once the OS has loaded, not at power-on beeps.","An expired certificate is an OS- or application-level issue, not a POST hardware fault.",null,"A full data volume is an OS-level problem and would not produce power-on beep codes."],2],["sv70",0,4,"A server shuts down on its own every afternoon, and the BMC log shows high inlet temperature events. What is the most likely cause?",["Cooling problems in the room or rack","A failing NIC","An OS memory leak","A bad DNS record"],0,"High inlet temperature means the air coming in is too warm, pointing to HVAC, blocked airflow or hot air recirculation. A NIC failure would not trigger thermal shutdown.","Objective 4.2",[null,"A failing NIC affects networking and would not trigger a thermal shutdown.","A memory leak causes gradual slowdown or crashes, not high inlet-temperature events.","A bad DNS record affects name resolution, not the server's temperature."],2],["sv71",0,4,"The OS reports corrected memory errors on one DIMM several times a day. What should the admin do?",["Ignore them since ECC corrected them","Schedule replacement of that DIMM","Increase swap space","Disable ECC in firmware"],1,"Rising correctable errors often lead to uncorrectable ones, so the DIMM should be replaced in a maintenance window. Disabling ECC removes protection.","Objective 4.2",["Ignoring rising correctable errors is risky because they often precede uncorrectable failures.",null,"Increasing swap space is disk paging and does nothing to fix a degrading DIMM.","Disabling ECC removes the very protection that is catching these errors."],2],["sv72",0,4,"One power supply in a redundant pair shows an amber LED, but the server is still running. What is the right action?",["Shut the server down immediately","Update the OS","Move all load to a Type 2 hypervisor","Replace the failed hot-swap PSU and check its power feed"],3,"Redundant PSUs keep the server running, but the failed unit (or its feed) must be fixed to restore redundancy. An immediate shutdown is unnecessary.","Objective 4.2",["Shutting down immediately is unnecessary because the redundant supply keeps the server running.","Updating the OS is irrelevant to a failed power supply.","Moving load to a Type 2 hypervisor makes no sense as a response to a PSU fault.",null],2],["sv73",0,4,"A RAID 5 array shows as degraded after one drive failed. What is the main risk until it is rebuilt?",["Slower DNS lookups","The OS license will expire","The array will automatically become RAID 0","A second drive failure will lose the array"],3,"RAID 5 tolerates only one failure; another failure before the rebuild finishes destroys the array. Replace the drive promptly and check backups.","Objective 4.3",["Slower DNS lookups are unrelated to the state of a degraded RAID array.","An OS license does not expire because an array is degraded.","A degraded RAID 5 array does not automatically convert itself to RAID 0.",null],2],["sv74",0,4,"After a power outage, a server shows 'boot device not found'. The drives spin up normally. What should the admin check first?",["The boot order and RAID controller configuration in firmware","The DNS server","The license key","The NIC teaming mode"],0,"A reset firmware or controller setting can change boot order or hide the array. DNS and licensing do not affect whether the disk is found at boot.","Objective 4.3",[null,"DNS plays no part in whether the firmware can locate a boot device.","A license key affects OS activation, not the hardware's ability to find the disk at boot.","NIC teaming is a network setting and has nothing to do with locating the boot drive."],3],["sv75",0,4,"SMART data for a drive shows a rising count of reallocated sectors. What does this indicate?",["The drive is healthy and optimizing","The drive is likely to fail and should be replaced","The file system needs defragmenting","The RAID controller cache is full"],1,"Reallocated sectors mean the drive is remapping bad areas; a growing count predicts failure. Defragmenting will not help.","Objective 4.3",["Reallocated sectors signal degradation, not a healthy drive optimizing itself.",null,"Defragmenting rearranges files and cannot repair physically bad sectors.","A full controller cache is unrelated to a drive's SMART reallocated-sector count."],2],["sv76",0,4,"A Linux server's application crashes with 'No space left on device', yet `df -h` shows 40% free. What should the admin check next?",["The DHCP lease","The default gateway","Inode usage with df -i","The SSH key"],2,"A volume can run out of inodes when it holds many small files, even with free blocks. `df -i` shows inode use.","Objective 4.3",["A DHCP lease concerns IP addressing and has nothing to do with a disk-space error.","The default gateway is a routing setting, unrelated to running out of space.",null,"An SSH key is for authentication and does not affect storage capacity."],3],["sv77",0,4,"After an unclean shutdown, a Linux data volume will not mount and reports file system errors. Which tool repairs it?",["chmod on the mount point","fsck on the unmounted volume","ping to the storage array","top"],1,"fsck checks and repairs Linux file systems and must be run while the volume is unmounted. chmod changes permissions only.","Objective 4.3",["chmod changes permissions on the mount point and does not repair a corrupted file system.",null,"ping only tests network reachability to the storage array, not file system integrity.","top displays running processes and cannot repair a file system."],2],["sv78",0,4,"Disk write latency on a RAID controller jumped after the controller's battery failed. What most likely happened?",["The array changed RAID level","The NIC dropped to 100 Mbps","The drives were set to read-only","The controller switched from write-back to write-through cache"],3,"Without a healthy battery or flash backup, controllers disable write-back caching to protect data, so writes go straight to disk and slow down.","Objective 4.3",["A failed cache battery does not change the array's RAID level.","The NIC speed is a network matter and is unrelated to disk write latency.","A dead cache battery does not set the drives to read-only.",null],3],["sv79",0,4,"A Windows server's available memory drops steadily over two weeks until it slows, and a restart fixes it for a while. What is the most likely cause?",["A memory leak in a process","A failed power supply","A duplex mismatch","An incorrect subnet mask"],0,"Gradual memory loss that a reboot clears is typical of a memory leak. Trending process memory in Performance Monitor finds the culprit.","Objective 4.4",[null,"A failed power supply causes an outage, not a slow, steady decline in available memory.","A duplex mismatch degrades network performance, not memory availability.","An incorrect subnet mask affects connectivity, not the server's memory usage."],2],["sv80",0,4,"A Windows service fails to start after the service account's password was changed. What is the most likely fix?",["Reinstall the OS","Change the service to manual","Update the password stored in the service's Log On settings","Add more RAM"],2,"Services running under a domain account keep a copy of the password, which must be updated after a change. Reinstalling is excessive.","Objective 4.4",["Reinstalling the OS is far more work than needed to fix a changed service-account password.","Setting the service to manual changes its startup type but does not fix the wrong stored password.",null,"Adding RAM is unrelated to a service failing due to a credential change."],2],["sv81",0,4,"After a monthly patch, a critical application on a server stops working. Other servers without the patch are fine. What should the admin do first?",["Change the server's IP address","Disable the host firewall","Replace the server","Roll back the patch per the change plan and test"],3,"The patch is the likely cause, so rolling it back restores service while the issue is researched. Disabling the firewall adds risk and does not address the change.","Objective 4.4",["Changing the server's IP address is unrelated to a failure caused by a patch.","Disabling the host firewall adds risk and does not address the offending patch.","Replacing the server is drastic when the recent patch is the obvious suspect.",null],3],["sv82",0,4,"A Linux service fails to start. Which command shows its recent log messages?",["journalctl -u <service>","ip route","df -h","lsblk"],0,"journalctl with -u filters the systemd journal for that unit, showing why it failed. lsblk lists block devices.","Objective 4.4",[null,"ip route displays the routing table, not a service's log messages.","df -h shows disk space usage, not why a service failed.","lsblk lists block devices and reveals nothing about a service's logs."],1],["sv83",0,4,"A server can reach other hosts on its subnet but nothing on remote networks. What is the most likely cause?",["A wrong DNS server","A wrong or missing default gateway","A failed RAID array","An expired certificate"],1,"Traffic to other networks goes through the default gateway; if it is wrong, only local hosts work. DNS affects name resolution, not reachability by IP.","Objective 4.5",["A wrong DNS server breaks name resolution but not reachability to remote networks by IP.",null,"A failed RAID array is a storage issue and does not affect network reachability.","An expired certificate is an application-security problem, not a routing failure."],2],["sv84",0,4,"Users can reach a server by its IP address but not by its name. Which tool should the admin use first?",["arp -a","tracert","nslookup or dig","netstat -r"],2,"Name failure with working IP access points to DNS, which nslookup or dig tests directly. tracert tests the path, not naming.","Objective 4.5",["arp -a shows the local IP-to-MAC cache and does not test DNS name resolution.","tracert maps the network path by hops, not whether a name resolves.",null,"netstat -r prints the routing table, which is unrelated to name resolution."],2],["sv85",0,4,"A web app on a server is unreachable from clients, though ping works. The admin wants to test whether TCP port 443 is open from a client. Which command fits?",["arp -d","ipconfig /flushdns","Test-NetConnection server -Port 443","chkdsk /f"],2,"Test-NetConnection (or telnet/nc) attempts a TCP connection to a port, showing whether the service or firewall blocks it. flushdns only clears the name cache.","Objective 4.5",["arp -d clears the ARP cache and does not test whether a TCP port is open.","ipconfig /flushdns clears the DNS cache and does not probe a port.",null,"chkdsk /f checks a disk for errors and has nothing to do with testing a port."],3],["sv86",0,4,"A server's link is slow and the switch port shows many late collisions, while the server side shows none. What is the most likely cause?",["A duplex mismatch","DNS misconfiguration","A wrong VLAN","A bad gateway"],0,"Late collisions on one side mean one end is half duplex and the other full. Set both ends to match or to autonegotiate.","Objective 4.5",[null,"A DNS misconfiguration would break name resolution, not cause late collisions on a link.","A wrong VLAN would isolate traffic rather than produce late collisions.","A bad gateway is a routing problem and does not generate collisions."],3],["sv87",0,4,"A server with a static IP shows 'duplicate IP address' warnings after a new printer was added. What is the most likely cause?",["The server's clock is wrong","The server's NIC is failing","The DNS zone is corrupt","The printer got the server's address from the DHCP pool"],3,"If a static address sits inside the DHCP scope, a new device can be leased the same address. Exclude or reserve static addresses.","Objective 4.5",["A wrong clock is a time-sync issue and does not create a duplicate IP address.","A failing NIC would cause link problems, not a duplicate-address warning.","A corrupt DNS zone affects name resolution, not IP address conflicts.",null],3],["sv88",0,4,"After a server's TLS certificate expired, clients see security warnings. What should the admin do?",["Disable TLS on the server","Renew and install a new certificate, and set a renewal reminder","Change the server's IP address","Restart the DNS service"],1,"An expired certificate must be replaced; tracking or automating renewal prevents recurrence. Disabling TLS removes encryption.","Objective 4.6",["Disabling TLS removes encryption entirely and leaves clients unprotected.",null,"Changing the server's IP address has no bearing on an expired certificate.","Restarting the DNS service does not renew or replace an expired certificate."],2],["sv89",0,4,"A user can open a shared folder but cannot save files, even though the share permission is Full Control. What is the most likely cause?",["The DNS record is wrong","The NTFS permissions only allow Read","The server has no default gateway","The disk is in RAID 1"],1,"Share and NTFS permissions combine and the more restrictive applies, so Read on NTFS blocks writes. DNS problems would block opening the share at all.","Objective 4.6",["A wrong DNS record would prevent reaching the share at all, not just block saving files.",null,"A missing gateway would block access entirely, yet the user can already open the share.","RAID 1 is disk redundancy and is not a permission control that would block writes."],3],["sv90",0,4,"After installing new endpoint protection, a line-of-business app on a server stops launching. What is the most likely cause?",["The NIC team split","The RAID battery failed","The app's files were quarantined or blocked by the security software","The DHCP scope is exhausted"],2,"New antivirus or EDR can flag legitimate executables; check its quarantine and add a vetted exclusion. The other issues would not appear right after that install.","Objective 4.6",["A split NIC team is a network issue and would not stop an application from launching.","A failed RAID battery slows disk writes but would not block an app right after an AV install.",null,"An exhausted DHCP scope affects addressing, not an app blocked following an EDR install."],2],["x1",0,1,"A 1U server on sliding rails must sometimes be pulled forward for service while it keeps running. What keeps its power and network cables from being strained or unplugged as it slides out?",["Velcro ties bundling the cables tightly to the rear rack post","Shorter patch and power cords so there is no slack to snag","A cable management arm attached to the rear of the rails","A vertical PDU mounted directly behind the server"],2,"A cable management arm folds and extends with the server, so cables follow it as it slides out. Tying cables tightly to the post is the tempting choice, but it removes the slack the server needs and pulls connectors loose.","Domain 1: Rack planning and cable management",["Tying cables tightly to the post removes the slack the server needs, so sliding it out pulls connectors loose.","Shorter cords make the problem worse; with no slack the cables unplug as soon as the server moves.",null,"A PDU is a power source, not a way to guide moving cables, and does nothing for network cabling."],2],["x2",0,1,"A high-power blade chassis power supply has a C20 inlet. Which connector must be on the end of the power cord that plugs into it?",["C19","C13","C14","NEMA 5-15P"],0,"IEC C19 connectors mate with C20 inlets and are used for higher-current equipment. C13 is the common tempting answer, but C13 mates with the smaller C14 inlet found on typical server PSUs.","Domain 1: Power connectors",[null,"C13 connectors mate with C14 inlets, which are the smaller, lower-current type on typical server PSUs.","C14 is itself an inlet style; a cord end must be the matching connector, and C14 does not fit a C20.","NEMA 5-15P is a North American wall plug, not an IEC connector that fits an equipment inlet."],1],["x3",0,1,"Two data halls about 8 km apart need a 10 Gbps link between their core switches. Which transceiver and cabling pairing fits?",["SFP+ short-reach optics over OM3 multimode fiber","SFP+ long-reach optics over single-mode fiber","A passive twinax DAC cable run between the halls","10GBASE-T ports over shielded Cat6a copper"],1,"Long-reach 10 Gb optics over single-mode fiber are designed for distances of several kilometers. Short-reach optics over OM3 multimode are tempting because they are cheaper, but they only reach a few hundred meters.","Domain 1: Fiber types and transceivers",["Short-reach multimode optics reach only a few hundred meters, far short of 8 km.",null,"Passive DAC cables are only meant for a few meters, such as within or between adjacent racks.","10GBASE-T over Cat6a is limited to about 100 meters of copper."],2],["x4",0,1,"An archive server will store rarely accessed video files. Capacity per dollar matters most and performance needs are low. Which drives fit best?",["15K RPM SAS drives in small form factor bays","NVMe SSDs installed in U.2 hot-swap bays","10K RPM SAS drives in 2.5-inch carriers","7.2K RPM high-capacity nearline drives"],3,"7.2K RPM nearline drives give the most capacity for the money and are fine for cold, sequential archive data. 10K RPM SAS drives are tempting as a middle ground, but they cost more per terabyte and offer less capacity for no real benefit here.","Domain 1: Drive types and speeds",["15K SAS drives are fast but expensive and low capacity, wasted on rarely read archive data.","NVMe SSDs cost far more per terabyte and their speed is not needed for cold archive storage.","10K SAS drives still cost more per terabyte than nearline drives with little benefit for archives.",null],2],["x5",0,1,"A write-heavy database was placed on RAID 6. Compared with RAID 10 on the same drives, why is its write performance noticeably worse?",["Each small write must read and update two parity blocks, costing extra disk I/O","RAID 6 stripes data across fewer disks than RAID 10 does","RAID 6 mirrors every block to three disks before confirming","RAID 6 cannot be used with a controller's write-back cache"],0,"RAID 6 keeps dual parity, so every small write triggers extra reads and writes to update both parity blocks (a high write penalty). The mirroring answer is wrong because RAID 6 uses parity, not mirroring; RAID 10 is the level that mirrors.","Domain 1: RAID write penalty",[null,"RAID 6 and RAID 10 can span the same number of disks; the difference is dual parity, not stripe width.","RAID 6 uses parity, not mirroring; RAID 10 is the level that mirrors blocks.","RAID 6 works fine with write-back cache, which actually helps hide its write penalty."],2],["x6",0,1,"A lab server has four drives of different sizes. The admin wants them presented as one large volume using all of their capacity and accepts that there is no redundancy. Which configuration fits?",["RAID 0 striping across all four drives","RAID 1 mirroring in two pairs","JBOD spanning (concatenation)","RAID 5 with one drive as parity"],2,"JBOD spanning joins drives end to end, so every drive's full capacity is used even when sizes differ. RAID 0 is tempting because it also has no redundancy, but it limits each member to the size of the smallest drive.","Domain 1: JBOD and RAID",["RAID 0 limits every member to the smallest drive's size, wasting capacity on the larger drives.","Mirroring halves capacity and pairs of different sizes waste more; it adds redundancy that was not required.",null,"RAID 5 gives up one drive's worth of capacity to parity and is limited by the smallest drive."],2],["x7",0,1,"Several departments need shared folders reached over SMB and NFS, and the storage device itself should manage the file system. Which storage type fits?",["An iSCSI target presenting block LUNs","A NAS appliance","A Fibre Channel SAN array","A DAS enclosure attached over SAS"],1,"A NAS owns the file system and serves files over SMB and NFS to many clients. An iSCSI target is tempting because it also uses Ethernet, but it serves raw blocks, leaving each server to manage its own file system.","Domain 1: Storage architectures",["An iSCSI target serves raw blocks; each server would manage its own file system, not the device.",null,"A Fibre Channel SAN also presents block storage and does not serve SMB or NFS shares itself.","DAS attaches to one server over SAS and does not share files over the network by itself."],2],["x8",0,1,"A company wants to carry Fibre Channel storage traffic over its existing 10 GbE data center network using converged adapters, without wrapping it in TCP/IP. Which protocol fits?",["iSCSI over a dedicated storage VLAN","FCIP tunneling between the switches","SMB Direct over RDMA-capable NICs","FCoE"],3,"FCoE encapsulates Fibre Channel frames directly in Ethernet frames on a lossless network, with no IP layer. FCIP is tempting because it also carries FC traffic, but it tunnels FC over TCP/IP, usually to link distant SANs.","Domain 1: FCoE and SAN protocols",["iSCSI wraps SCSI commands in TCP/IP, which is exactly the IP encapsulation the stem rules out.","FCIP tunnels Fibre Channel over TCP/IP, usually to join distant SANs, so it still uses IP.","SMB Direct is a file-sharing protocol over RDMA, not a way to carry Fibre Channel frames.",null],3],["x9",0,1,"A server room has 20 servers with no licensed BMC features. Admins want to see and control any server's console from their desks, including firmware setup screens. What fits?",["An IP KVM switch connected to each server's video and USB ports","A crash cart kept in the server room","Remote Desktop enabled on every server","SSH access through a hardened jump host"],0,"An IP KVM captures the actual video and keyboard signals, so it shows BIOS/UEFI screens remotely. RDP and SSH are tempting, but they need a running OS and cannot show POST or firmware setup; a crash cart requires being physically present.","Domain 1: Out-of-band management",[null,"A crash cart needs someone physically in the server room, which does not let admins work from their desks.","Remote Desktop requires a running Windows OS and cannot show POST or firmware setup screens.","SSH also depends on a running OS and network stack, so it cannot show firmware screens."],3],["x10",0,1,"An admin wants BitLocker on a server's OS volume to unlock automatically at boot only if the early boot components have not been tampered with. Which hardware component provides this?",["A BMC with virtual media support","A self-encrypting SAS drive","A Trusted Platform Module (TPM)","The UEFI Compatibility Support Module"],2,"A TPM stores the key and releases it only when boot measurements match the expected values, enabling automatic but tamper-aware unlock. A self-encrypting drive is tempting, but it encrypts in hardware without measuring the boot chain.","Domain 1: TPM and firmware security",["A BMC's virtual media mounts ISO images remotely; it does not measure boot components or release keys.","A self-encrypting drive encrypts in hardware but does not measure the boot chain for tampering.",null,"CSM enables legacy BIOS booting and disables Secure Boot; it provides no key storage or measurements."],2],["x11",0,1,"A server with 24 DIMM slots will be fully populated with high-capacity modules. The vendor requires modules with a register chip that buffers command and address signals so more DIMMs can share a channel. Which type is this?",["Unbuffered ECC UDIMMs with standard timings","Registered DIMMs (RDIMMs)","SO-DIMMs","Non-ECC desktop DIMMs"],1,"Registered DIMMs add a register that buffers command and address lines, reducing electrical load so servers can run many modules per channel. Unbuffered ECC DIMMs are tempting because they also correct errors, but they lack the register and support fewer modules per channel.","Domain 1: Server memory types",["Unbuffered ECC DIMMs correct errors but lack the register, so fewer can share a channel.",null,"SO-DIMMs are small modules for laptops and compact systems, not high-density server boards.","Non-ECC desktop DIMMs lack both error correction and the register servers rely on."],1],["x12",0,2,"Which Windows Server file system is built for resilience, using checksums and integrity streams, and is commonly used for Hyper-V and Storage Spaces volumes?",["NTFS","exFAT","FAT32","ReFS"],3,"ReFS (Resilient File System) adds checksums, integrity streams and corruption repair with Storage Spaces. NTFS is tempting as the default Windows file system, but it does not provide ReFS's integrity checking.","Domain 2: File systems",["NTFS is the default Windows file system but lacks ReFS integrity streams and automatic repair.","exFAT is a lightweight file system for removable media with no integrity checking.","FAT32 is a legacy file system with a small file size limit and no resilience features.",null],1],["x13",0,2,"An admin formats a shared LUN so that several VMware ESXi hosts can store and run VMs from it at the same time. Which file system is used?",["VMFS","ext4","XFS","ReFS"],0,"VMFS is VMware's clustered file system, allowing multiple ESXi hosts to access the same datastore concurrently. XFS is tempting because it handles large files well, but it is a Linux file system and not what ESXi uses for shared block datastores.","Domain 2: File systems and virtualization",[null,"ext4 is a Linux file system and is not a clustered datastore that ESXi hosts share.","XFS handles large files well on Linux but is not used by ESXi for shared datastores.","ReFS is a Windows file system used with Hyper-V, not VMware ESXi datastores."],1],["x14",0,2,"An admin installs Windows Server on 15 physical hosts from media and wants every install to choose the same partitions, locale and admin settings with no prompts. What should be used?",["A P2V conversion tool","A VM template on the hypervisor","An unattend.xml answer file","A DHCP reservation for each host"],2,"An answer file supplies the responses to setup prompts so each installation runs unattended and identically. A VM template is tempting for standard builds, but these are physical hosts installed from media, not VMs.","Domain 2: Installation methods",["P2V converts an existing physical server into a VM; it does not automate fresh installations.","A VM template only helps deploy VMs on a hypervisor, but these are physical hosts installed from media.",null,"A DHCP reservation fixes an IP address; it does not answer any setup prompts."],2],["x15",0,2,"An admin will clone a configured Windows Server VM to create five new domain members. What should be done to the source VM before it is captured as the image?",["Join it to the domain first so the clones inherit membership","Generalize it with Sysprep","Take a snapshot and then delete the parent disk","Assign it a static IP so each clone keeps it"],1,"Sysprep generalizes the image, removing machine-specific identity so each clone gets a unique identity. Joining the domain first is tempting, but every clone would share the same computer account and identity, causing conflicts.","Domain 2: Imaging and cloning",["Joining first would give every clone the same computer identity and account, causing domain conflicts.",null,"Deleting a parent disk breaks the snapshot chain and does nothing to make the image unique.","Every clone keeping the same static IP would cause address conflicts on the network."],2],["x16",0,2,"A server's network interface shows an IPv6 address beginning with fe80:: and no other IPv6 address. What does this indicate?",["The server obtained a globally routable address through SLAAC","The interface has joined an IPv6 multicast group for routers","The interface is using the IPv6 loopback address only","Only a link-local address exists, usable on the local segment"],3,"Addresses in fe80::/10 are link-local; every IPv6 interface creates one automatically, and they cannot be routed. A SLAAC global address is tempting, but it would start with a global prefix, not fe80.","Domain 2: IPv6 addressing",["A SLAAC global unicast address would start with a routable global prefix, not fe80.","IPv6 multicast addresses start with ff, not fe80.","The IPv6 loopback address is ::1, not an fe80:: address.",null],1],["x17",0,2,"A new server will provide LDAP over TLS (LDAPS) for directory lookups. Which inbound TCP port must its host firewall allow?",["636","389","3389","443"],0,"LDAPS uses TCP 636 by default. Port 389 is the tempting choice, but that is standard LDAP, which is unencrypted unless upgraded with StartTLS.","Domain 2: Network services and ports",[null,"Port 389 is plain LDAP, which is unencrypted unless upgraded with StartTLS.","Port 3389 is Remote Desktop Protocol, not a directory service.","Port 443 is HTTPS for web traffic, not LDAPS."],1],["x18",0,2,"A Linux server bonds two NICs in 802.3ad (LACP) mode. The bond reports no LACP partner and throughput is poor. The two switch ports are plain access ports. What is missing?",["A second default gateway on the bond interface","Jumbo frames enabled on both physical NICs","A matching LACP port channel on the switch ports","A separate IP address configured on each member NIC"],2,"LACP is a negotiated protocol, so the switch must have the two ports in a matching LACP port channel. Separate IPs per member is tempting, but bonding places the IP on the bond itself, and it would not create an LACP partner.","Domain 2: NIC teaming and bonding",["A bond uses one default gateway like any interface; a second gateway does not create an LACP partner.","Jumbo frames change frame size but have nothing to do with LACP negotiation.",null,"In bonding, the IP address lives on the bond interface, not on each member NIC."],3],["x19",0,2,"An online store keeps shopping carts in each web server's local memory. Behind a round-robin load balancer, users randomly lose their carts. Which load balancer setting fixes this without changing the app?",["Switch the pool to the least connections algorithm","Enable session persistence (sticky sessions)","Configure active-passive failover","Run health checks every five seconds"],1,"Session persistence sends each user back to the same server, where the cart lives in memory. Least connections is tempting because it balances better, but it still moves users between servers and loses their carts.","Domain 2: Load balancing",["Least connections still sends a user's requests to different servers, so carts are still lost.",null,"Active-passive failover uses only one server at a time and changes the design rather than fixing sessions.","Health checks remove dead servers but do not keep a user on the server holding their cart."],2],["x20",0,2,"In an active-passive cluster the primary node failed and services moved to the secondary. The primary is now repaired. What is moving the services back to it called?",["Failover","Heartbeat","Split-brain","Failback"],3,"Failback returns services to the original primary after it is restored. Failover is tempting, but that term describes the original move away from the failed node.","Domain 2: High availability",["Failover is the move away from the failed primary, not the return to it.","The heartbeat is the signal nodes use to check each other's health.","Split-brain is a fault where both nodes believe they are active, not a planned move.",null],1],["x21",0,2,"Which statement best distinguishes fault tolerance from high availability?",["Fault tolerance continues with no interruption when a part fails; HA may allow a brief outage during failover","Fault tolerance relies on clustering whole servers, while HA relies only on redundant parts inside one server","Fault tolerance protects against data loss only, while HA protects against every kind of downtime","Fault tolerance is a software-only feature, while HA always requires specialized redundant hardware"],0,"A fault-tolerant design keeps running through a component failure with no interruption, while HA minimizes downtime but can have a short pause during failover. The clustering answer is tempting but reversed: clustering is a typical HA method.","Domain 2: Redundancy vs high availability",[null,"This is reversed: clustering whole servers is a typical HA method, and redundant parts support fault tolerance.","Fault tolerance is about continuing operation through component failure, not only preventing data loss.","Fault tolerance is usually achieved with redundant hardware, so calling it software-only is wrong."],2],["x22",0,2,"A latency-sensitive database VM slows during peak hours. The host's CPUs are fully busy and the VM shows high CPU ready time. What is the best fix?",["Add more vCPUs to the VM so it can do more work in parallel","Increase the VM's memory allocation","Reduce CPU overcommitment on the host or move the VM to a less busy host","Convert the VM's virtual disk from thin to thick provisioning"],2,"High ready time means the VM is waiting for physical CPU, so the host is overcommitted; freeing CPU or moving the VM fixes it. Adding vCPUs is tempting, but on a busy host it makes scheduling harder and usually increases ready time.","Domain 2: Virtualization resource allocation",["More vCPUs make the VM harder to schedule on a busy host and usually raise ready time further.","The symptom is CPU ready time, which points to CPU contention, not a memory shortage.",null,"Disk provisioning type affects storage allocation, not the CPU scheduling delay shown here."],3],["x23",0,2,"On a Hyper-V host, a group of test VMs must talk to each other but must never reach the physical network or the host itself. Which virtual switch type fits?",["External","Private","Internal","External with VLAN tagging"],1,"A private virtual switch connects only the VMs on it, with no host or physical network access. An internal switch is tempting, but it also connects the host OS to the VMs.","Domain 2: Virtual switches",["An external switch connects VMs to the physical network, which the stem forbids.",null,"An internal switch also connects the host OS to the VMs, which the stem forbids.","Adding VLAN tagging to an external switch still leaves VMs on the physical network."],2],["x24",0,2,"An admin must move running VMs off a host for hardware maintenance without downtime. Both hosts use the same shared storage. What should be used?",["Cold migration after shutting each VM down","A P2V conversion of each VM","Restoring each VM from last night's backup","Live migration"],3,"Live migration (such as vMotion or Hyper-V Live Migration) moves a running VM's memory and state to another host with no noticeable downtime. Cold migration is tempting because it also moves VMs, but it requires shutting them down.","Domain 2: VM migration",["Cold migration needs each VM shut down, which causes the downtime the admin must avoid.","P2V converts physical servers into VMs; these are already VMs.","Restoring from backup causes downtime and loses changes made since last night.",null],1],["x25",0,2,"Developers want to deploy web code without patching operating systems or installing runtimes; the provider manages the platform underneath. Which cloud service model fits?",["PaaS","IaaS","SaaS","Colocation in a private cloud"],0,"In PaaS the provider manages the OS and runtime, and customers deploy their own code. IaaS is tempting, but there the customer still manages and patches the OS.","Domain 2: Cloud models",[null,"With IaaS the customer still installs, patches and manages the OS and runtime.","SaaS delivers a finished application; developers cannot deploy their own code to it.","Colocation means hosting your own hardware in someone else's facility, so you still manage everything."],1],["x26",0,2,"A company keeps its customer database on-premises for compliance but runs its public website on a cloud provider, with the two environments connected. Which deployment model is this?",["Public cloud","Private cloud","Hybrid cloud","Community cloud"],2,"Hybrid cloud combines on-premises or private resources with public cloud services that work together. Public cloud is tempting because the website is there, but the database stays on-premises, making the whole environment hybrid.","Domain 2: Cloud deployment models",["Only the website is in the public cloud; the database stays on-premises, so it is not purely public.","A private cloud is dedicated infrastructure, but here a public provider hosts part of the environment.",null,"A community cloud is shared by organizations with common needs, which is not described here."],1],["x27",0,2,"A Bash script must print a warning when the variable USED holds a number greater than 90. Which test is correct?",["if ($USED -gt 90) {","if [ \"$USED\" -gt 90 ]; then","if [ \"$USED\" > 90 ]; then","if [ \"$USED\" -greater 90 ]; then"],1,"In Bash, the test command uses -gt for numeric greater-than inside [ ], followed by then. Using > is tempting, but inside [ ] it is treated as output redirection and creates a file named 90 instead of comparing.","Domain 2: Scripting basics",["This is PowerShell syntax with parentheses and braces; Bash would not parse it as a condition.",null,"Inside [ ], > is treated as output redirection and creates a file named 90 instead of comparing.","-greater is not a valid test operator; Bash uses -gt for numeric greater-than."],2],["x28",0,2,"A Windows admin needs a PowerShell script to run every weekday at 6:00 AM under a service account, whether or not anyone is logged on. What should be used?",["A cron entry on the server","A Group Policy logon script","A Windows service set to Automatic start","Task Scheduler"],3,"Task Scheduler runs scripts on a schedule under a chosen account without anyone signed in. A logon script is tempting, but it only runs when a user signs in, not at a set time.","Domain 2: Scripting and scheduled tasks",["cron is the Linux scheduler and is not part of Windows Server.","A logon script runs only when a user signs in, not at 6:00 AM with no one logged on.","A service runs continuously, not on a weekday schedule, and a script is not written as a service.",null],2],["x29",0,2,"A vendor charges a yearly fee that includes updates and support, and the right to use the software ends when payments stop. Which licensing model is this?",["Subscription","Perpetual per-device","Site license","Per-socket"],0,"Subscription licensing grants use for as long as the recurring fee is paid, usually including updates. A perpetual license is tempting because it may also have yearly maintenance, but its right to use does not end when payments stop.","Domain 2: Licensing models",[null,"A perpetual license lets you keep using the software even if you stop paying for maintenance.","A site license covers all use at a location; it describes scope, not a recurring right to use.","Per-socket licensing counts processors; it says nothing about use ending when payments stop."],1],["x30",0,3,"Backups are copied each night from a branch server to head office over the internet. Which control protects the data while it crosses the network?",["Full-disk encryption on the branch server's volumes","Keeping the backup media in a locked cabinet","An encrypted tunnel such as a VPN or TLS","Compressing the backup files before sending them"],2,"Encryption in transit, such as a VPN or TLS, protects data as it travels over untrusted networks. Full-disk encryption is tempting, but it protects data at rest on the disk, not while it is being sent.","Domain 3: Data security in transit",["Full-disk encryption protects data at rest on the server, not while it crosses the internet.","A locked cabinet is physical security for media and does not protect network traffic.",null,"Compression shrinks the files but does not hide their contents from anyone intercepting them."],2],["x31",0,3,"A data center wants fire suppression that will not soak equipment with water and leaves no residue on electronics. Which option fits best?",["A wet-pipe sprinkler system","A clean agent gaseous suppression system","Dry chemical extinguishers mounted on each rack","A deluge sprinkler system"],1,"Clean agent gas systems suppress fire without water and leave no residue, protecting electronics. Dry chemical extinguishers are tempting because they avoid water, but the powder is corrosive and leaves residue on equipment.","Domain 3: Physical security and fire suppression",["Wet-pipe sprinklers discharge water that damages electronics.",null,"Dry chemical powder is corrosive and leaves residue that damages electronics.","A deluge system floods the area with water, the opposite of what is wanted."],2],["x32",0,3,"An application's service account was made a Domain Admin because it was easier. Which change best reduces risk while keeping the app working?",["Rename the account so attackers cannot guess it","Set its password to never expire to avoid outages","Add it to the local Administrators group on every server instead","Grant only the rights the app needs and deny interactive logon"],3,"Least privilege for service accounts means giving only the rights the application needs and blocking interactive use. Local admin on every server is tempting as a step down, but it still grants broad control across the whole estate.","Domain 3: Service accounts and least privilege",["Renaming the account is obscurity only; it keeps all the domain admin rights.","A never-expiring password increases risk and does not reduce the excess privileges.","Local admin on every server still grants broad control across the environment.",null],3],["x33",0,3,"Logs show thousands of password guesses against a single administrator account. Which policy most directly limits this attack?",["Account lockout after several failed attempts","A password history of 24 remembered passwords","A minimum password age of one day","Disabling the built-in Guest account"],0,"Account lockout stops or slows repeated guessing by locking the account after a set number of failures. Password history is tempting because it is a password policy, but it only prevents reuse of old passwords and does nothing against guessing.","Domain 3: Password and lockout policies",[null,"Password history only prevents reusing old passwords; it does nothing to stop guessing.","Minimum password age stops rapid password changes and does not limit guessing.","The Guest account is not the one being attacked, so disabling it does not stop these guesses."],2],["x34",0,3,"Which sign-in method is an example of multifactor authentication?",["A password plus the answer to a security question","A PIN plus a password","A password plus a code from an authenticator app","A fingerprint scan plus a palm scan"],2,"A password (something you know) plus an app code (something you have) combines two different factor types. The fingerprint and palm option is tempting because it uses two checks, but both are biometrics, the same factor type.","Domain 3: MFA",["A security question is another thing you know, the same factor as a password.","A PIN and a password are both things you know, so this is single-factor.",null,"Fingerprint and palm scans are both biometrics, the same factor type."],2],["x35",0,3,"Users complain about signing in separately to the intranet, the ticketing system and email. Which solution lets a single authentication grant access to all three?",["MFA on each application","Single sign-on (SSO)","A shared generic account per department","A stricter password complexity policy"],1,"SSO lets users authenticate once and then access multiple trusting applications. MFA is tempting because it is an authentication improvement, but it adds factors rather than reducing the number of sign-ins.","Domain 3: Identity and access management",["MFA adds factors to each sign-in rather than reducing how many times users sign in.",null,"Shared accounts break accountability and are a security risk; they are not SSO.","Stricter complexity rules make sign-ins harder and do not combine them."],1],["x36",0,3,"Confidential files vanished from a file share and nobody can tell who deleted them. What should have been enabled beforehand to answer that question?",["Volume shadow copies on the share","Read-only share permissions for everyone","BitLocker on the data volume","Object access auditing on the folder"],3,"Object access auditing records which account read, changed or deleted files. Shadow copies are tempting because they help recover the files, but they do not record who deleted them.","Domain 3: Auditing",["Shadow copies can restore the files but do not record which account deleted them.","Read-only permissions might have prevented deletion but give no record of who did what.","BitLocker protects data at rest from theft and does not log file deletions.",null],2],["x37",0,3,"Ransomware on one user PC spread to every server because all systems sat on one flat network. Which mitigation best limits this spread in future?",["Segment the network with firewall rules between user and server VLANs","Increase backup frequency from nightly to hourly","Add a second domain controller at the site","Enable NIC teaming on all servers"],0,"Segmentation limits which systems infected clients can reach, containing lateral movement. More frequent backups are tempting because they aid recovery, but they do nothing to stop the ransomware spreading.","Domain 3: Data security risks and segmentation",[null,"More frequent backups help recovery but do nothing to stop ransomware spreading.","A second domain controller adds directory redundancy but does not block lateral movement.","NIC teaming provides link redundancy and has no effect on malware spread."],3],["x38",0,3,"When hardening a server, how should its host firewall handle inbound traffic?",["Allow everything by default and block known malicious ports","Allow all traffic that comes from the internal network","Deny by default and allow only the ports its roles need","Turn it off because the perimeter firewall already filters traffic"],2,"A default-deny inbound policy with only required ports open shrinks the attack surface. Trusting all internal traffic is tempting, but threats often come from compromised internal hosts, so internal traffic still needs filtering.","Domain 3: Server hardening",["Allow-by-default leaves every unneeded service exposed, the opposite of hardening.","Trusting all internal traffic ignores compromised internal hosts, a common attack path.",null,"Relying only on the perimeter leaves the server exposed to anything already inside."],2],["x39",0,3,"A company wants to reuse hard drives from retired servers in its test lab rather than destroy them, while still sanitizing old data. Which method fits?",["Degaussing each drive","Overwriting every sector with a verified wipe tool","Shredding the drives and recycling the platters","Quick-formatting each drive"],1,"A verified overwrite removes the data while leaving the drive usable. Degaussing is tempting because it sanitizes magnetic media, but it also destroys the drive's servo information, so the drive cannot be reused.","Domain 3: Decommissioning and media sanitization",["Degaussing also erases the drive's servo tracks, leaving the drive unusable.",null,"Shredding destroys the drives, but the goal is to reuse them.","A quick format only rebuilds file system structures and leaves the old data recoverable."],3],["x40",0,3,"The backup window is too short to send a full backup over the network each week. The backup server can build a new full from the last full plus the incrementals it already holds. What is this?",["A differential backup taken each night","A storage snapshot","A mirror backup","A synthetic full backup"],3,"A synthetic full is assembled on the backup server from existing full and incremental backups, without re-reading the source. A differential is tempting, but it still copies changed data from the source and is not a new full.","Domain 3: Backup types",["A differential copies changed data from the source; it is not a new full backup built on the server.","A storage snapshot is a point-in-time image on the same storage, not a full backup built from incrementals.","A mirror backup copies the source as-is and still reads everything from the source.",null],2],["x41",0,3,"An admin argues that VM snapshots stored on the same datastore make separate backups unnecessary. Why is this wrong?",["Snapshots depend on the original disks, so losing that storage loses both","Snapshots can only be taken while the VM is fully powered off","Snapshots record only memory state and never capture any disk data","Snapshots are always deleted automatically by the hypervisor after a day"],0,"A snapshot is a delta that depends on the base disk on the same storage, so a datastore failure destroys both. The memory-only answer is tempting, but snapshots do capture disk state; the problem is that they are not independent copies.","Domain 3: Snapshots vs backups",[null,"Snapshots can be taken while VMs run; that is one of their main uses.","Snapshots do capture disk state (and optionally memory); the issue is their dependence on the base disks.","Hypervisors do not delete snapshots automatically after a day; they persist until removed."],3],["x42",0,3,"A company can tolerate a few days of downtime after a disaster. It wants hardware and network links ready at the recovery site but will restore current data from backups, keeping costs moderate. Which site type fits?",["Hot site","Cold site","Warm site","Reciprocal agreement with no set equipment"],2,"A warm site has equipment and connectivity in place but needs data restored, giving recovery in hours to days at moderate cost. A cold site is tempting because it is cheaper, but it lacks ready hardware and takes longer to bring up.","Domain 3: Disaster recovery sites",["A hot site keeps current data and can take over in minutes, at a much higher cost than needed.","A cold site has space and power but no ready hardware, so recovery takes longer than desired.",null,"A reciprocal agreement with no set equipment does not guarantee the ready hardware the stem requires."],3],["x43",0,3,"A server model fails on average once every 50,000 hours, and each repair takes about 4 hours. Which metric is the 4 hours?",["MTBF","MTTR","RTO","RPO"],1,"Mean time to repair (MTTR) is the average time to restore a failed component. RTO is tempting, but it is a business target for how quickly a service must be recovered, not a measured average repair time.","Domain 3: Business continuity metrics",["MTBF is the average time between failures, the 50,000 hours in this case.",null,"RTO is a business target for recovery time, not a measured average repair time.","RPO is the maximum acceptable data loss measured in time, unrelated to repair duration."],1],["x44",0,4,"A technician believes a failed NIC driver update caused a server outage. According to the CompTIA troubleshooting methodology, what should the technician do next?",["Test the theory to determine the cause","Implement the solution by reinstalling the driver","Document the findings and close the ticket","Verify full system functionality"],0,"After establishing a theory of probable cause, the next step is to test it to confirm the cause. Implementing the fix is tempting, but acting before confirming the theory risks changing the wrong thing.","Domain 4: Troubleshooting methodology",[null,"Implementing a fix before confirming the theory risks changing the wrong thing.","Documentation is the final step, after the fix is implemented and verified.","Verification comes after a solution is implemented, which has not happened yet."],2],["x45",0,4,"After replacing a failed RAID controller, the server boots and the array is healthy. According to the troubleshooting methodology, what comes next?",["Document findings and close the ticket","Establish a new theory of probable cause","Escalate the issue to the hardware vendor","Verify full system functionality and apply preventive measures"],3,"After implementing the solution, the technician verifies full functionality and applies preventive measures, then documents. Documenting is tempting, but it is the final step and comes after verification.","Domain 4: Troubleshooting methodology",["Documenting is the last step and comes after verifying full functionality.","The fix worked, so there is no need to form a new theory.","Escalation is for when a problem cannot be solved; this one has been fixed.",null],2],["x46",0,4,"A drive in a hot-swap bay shows an amber predictive-failure indicator, but the array is still optimal. What is the best action?",["Ignore it until the array reports as degraded","Replace the drive in a planned window before it fails","Rebuild the whole array from scratch using all new drives","Reseat the drive to clear the warning"],1,"A predictive-failure alert means the drive is likely to fail soon, so replacing it in a controlled way avoids running degraded. Reseating is tempting as a quick fix, but it only hides the warning and the underlying fault remains.","Domain 4: Hardware problems and indicators",["Waiting until the array degrades means running with no redundancy when a second fault could occur.",null,"Rebuilding the whole array is disruptive and unnecessary when only one drive is flagged.","Reseating only clears the indicator temporarily; the drive's underlying fault remains."],2],["x47",0,4,"After a fan in a 1U server failed, the remaining fans run at full speed and the BMC logs CPU temperature warnings. What should the admin do?",["Raise the BMC temperature thresholds so the alerts stop","Remove the blanking panels nearby to let in more cool air","Replace the failed fan module and check the airflow path","Raise CPU clock speeds so jobs finish faster"],2,"Replacing the failed fan restores designed cooling, and checking airflow catches other blockages. Removing blanking panels is tempting, but it lets hot exhaust recirculate to the front and makes cooling worse.","Domain 4: Overheating and fan failure",["Raising thresholds hides the warning and lets components run hotter until they fail.","Removing blanking panels lets hot exhaust recirculate to the front, making cooling worse.",null,"Higher clock speeds generate more heat, making an overheating problem worse."],1],["x48",0,4,"After adding memory, a server fails POST with a memory configuration error. The new modules are the same type as the existing ones. What is the most likely cause?",["The modules are not installed in the slot population order the vendor requires","The operating system lacks drivers for the new memory","The RAID controller's cache is too small for the new RAM","The UPS cannot supply enough power for the added modules"],0,"Servers require DIMMs in specific slots and channel patterns, and wrong placement causes POST configuration errors. OS drivers are tempting, but POST happens before the OS loads, so drivers cannot be the cause.","Domain 4: POST and memory errors",[null,"POST runs before any OS loads, so OS drivers cannot cause a POST memory error.","The RAID controller's cache is separate from system memory and has no role in memory POST checks.","A power shortfall would cause shutdowns or failed power-on, not a memory configuration error."],2],["x49",0,4,"During a rebuild of a degraded RAID 5 array built from large drives, the rebuild fails with read errors on a surviving disk and the array is lost. Which configuration would better have survived this?",["RAID 0, because it rebuilds faster","JBOD, so each disk stands alone","RAID 5 with additional member drives","RAID 6, which tolerates a second failure during rebuild"],3,"RAID 6's second parity lets the array survive another disk error while rebuilding. Adding drives to RAID 5 is tempting, but it makes rebuilds longer and exposes more disks to read errors while still tolerating only one failure.","Domain 4: RAID rebuild failures",["RAID 0 has no redundancy at all and would have been lost at the first drive failure.","JBOD has no redundancy, so any failed disk loses its data.","More RAID 5 members make rebuilds longer and still tolerate only one failure.",null],3],["x50",0,4,"Drives from a failed server were moved into an identical replacement. The RAID controller sees them but shows the array as a foreign configuration. How should the admin access the data?",["Initialize the virtual disk so it becomes usable","Import the foreign configuration in the controller utility","Run chkdsk against each physical drive","Clear the foreign configuration and rebuild the array"],1,"Importing the foreign configuration adopts the RAID metadata stored on the disks and brings the array online with data intact. Clearing and rebuilding is tempting because it removes the warning, but it discards the metadata and loses the array.","Domain 4: RAID controller troubleshooting",["Initializing writes a new empty volume and destroys the existing data.",null,"chkdsk works on file systems through the OS and cannot see an array the controller has not brought online.","Clearing the foreign configuration discards the RAID metadata and loses the array."],3],["x51",0,4,"A Windows server's data volume shows file system corruption after a sudden power loss. Which tool should be used to repair it?",["sfc /scannow","diskpart clean","chkdsk /f on the volume","defrag with the /x option"],2,"chkdsk /f checks and repairs file system structures on a volume. sfc /scannow is tempting, but it repairs protected Windows system files, not file system corruption on a data volume.","Domain 4: Storage tools",["sfc /scannow repairs protected Windows system files, not file system corruption on a data volume.","diskpart clean wipes the partition table and destroys the data.",null,"Defragmentation reorganizes files and does not repair corruption; it may worsen damage."],1],["x52",0,4,"Users say a file server is slow. Performance Monitor shows disk queue length consistently high, while CPU and memory use are low. Where is the bottleneck?",["The storage subsystem","The processors","The installed physical memory","The DNS server"],0,"A long, sustained disk queue with idle CPU and free memory points to storage that cannot keep up with I/O requests. Memory is tempting because low RAM can cause paging, but memory use is low here, so paging is not driving the disk load.","Domain 4: Performance baselines and slow I/O",[null,"CPU use is low, so the processors are not what is slowing the server.","Memory use is low, so heavy paging is not what is loading the disks.","DNS affects name lookups, not file server disk performance."],2],["x53",0,4,"A Linux server's load average is very high. top shows one stuck backup script using 100% CPU for hours. What should the admin do?",["Reboot the server right away","Run fsck against the root file system","Renice the process to -20","Send it SIGTERM with kill, using kill -9 only if it ignores that"],3,"Terminating the runaway process frees the CPU, and SIGTERM first lets it clean up before a forced kill. Renicing to -20 is tempting, but -20 is the highest priority, which would make the process consume even more CPU.","Domain 4: Runaway processes",["Rebooting disrupts every service on the server when only one process needs to be stopped.","fsck checks file systems; the problem is a runaway process, not disk corruption.","A nice value of -20 gives the highest priority, making the process take even more CPU.",null],3],["x54",0,4,"After a new storage driver was installed, a Windows server restarts repeatedly before reaching the sign-in screen. What should the admin try first?",["Reinstall Windows Server from media","Boot into Safe Mode or recovery and roll back the driver","Replace the system board","Update the BIOS to the newest version"],1,"Safe Mode or the recovery environment loads a minimal driver set, allowing the new driver to be rolled back. Reinstalling the OS is tempting because it would clear the fault, but it is far more disruptive than undoing the one recent change.","Domain 4: Boot loops and driver issues",["Reinstalling the OS is far more disruptive than undoing the single recent driver change.",null,"The fault began right after a driver install, which points to software, not a failed board.","A BIOS update is an unrelated change and adds risk without addressing the new driver."],2],["x55",0,4,"A server at 10.1.20.15 with mask 255.255.0.0 cannot reach hosts in 10.1.30.0/24. Other servers on 10.1.20.0/24 using mask 255.255.255.0 can. What is the cause?",["The default gateway on the server is set to 10.1.20.1","The DNS server does not have records for 10.1.30.0/24","The mask is too broad, so the server treats remote hosts as local and skips the gateway","The server's NIC is running at 100 Mbps instead of 1 Gbps"],2,"With a /16 mask the server thinks 10.1.30.x is on its own segment, so it tries ARP directly instead of sending to the router. The gateway answer is tempting, but the other working servers presumably use that same gateway; the wrong mask means it is never used.","Domain 4: Network configuration problems",["A gateway of 10.1.20.1 is normal for this subnet; with a /16 mask the server never uses it for 10.1.30.x.","The problem is reaching hosts, not resolving names; DNS records do not affect routing.",null,"Link speed affects throughput, not whether the server can reach another subnet at all."],3],["x56",0,4,"An admin wants to confirm that a Linux web service is listening on TCP port 8443 on the server itself. Which command fits?",["ss -tlnp","arp -a","traceroute","dig"],0,"ss -tlnp lists listening TCP sockets with numeric ports and the owning process. traceroute is tempting as a network tool, but it maps the route to a remote host and says nothing about local listening ports.","Domain 4: Network tools",[null,"arp -a shows IP-to-MAC mappings for neighbors, not listening ports.","traceroute maps the path to a remote host and says nothing about local listening sockets.","dig queries DNS and does not show which ports are listening."],1],["x57",0,4,"A Windows server's two NICs were placed in a switch-independent team, and the network team then configured a static link aggregation group on the matching switch ports. Connectivity is now intermittent. What is the fix?",["Assign a different IP address to each teamed NIC","Enable jumbo frames on the team interface","Replace both network cables","Make both sides match, either static/LACP teaming with the LAG or no LAG"],3,"Teaming mode must match the switch: a switch-independent team should connect to ordinary ports, while a LAG needs a switch-dependent (static or LACP) team. Replacing cables is tempting for intermittent faults, but the symptom here follows the config change, not a physical fault.","Domain 4: NIC teaming misconfiguration",["Teamed NICs share one team interface address; giving each its own IP does not fix the mode mismatch.","Jumbo frames change frame size and do not address the teaming and LAG mismatch.","The fault began after the configuration change, not a physical fault, so cables are unlikely to be the issue.",null],3],["x58",0,4,"A new SQL Server instance works locally, but remote clients time out connecting to TCP 1433. Ping by name works and the service is running. What is the most likely cause?",["DNS returns the wrong address for the server","The host firewall is blocking inbound TCP 1433","A duplex mismatch on the server's switch port","The server's TLS certificate has recently expired"],1,"Timeouts to one port while ping and the service work point to a firewall dropping that port. DNS is tempting, but ping by name already resolves and reaches the server, so name resolution is working.","Domain 4: Firewall and port problems",["Ping by name already resolves to and reaches the server, so DNS is working.",null,"A duplex mismatch causes slowness and errors, not a clean timeout on one port while ping works.","TLS certificate errors produce handshake warnings, not TCP connection timeouts."],2],["x59",0,4,"A user belongs to Sales, which has NTFS Modify on a folder, and to Contractors, which has an explicit Deny Write on the same folder. The user cannot save files there. Why?",["Share permissions always override the folder's NTFS permissions","The most recently added group's permissions win","An explicit deny overrides the allow from the other group","Modify does not include the ability to create files"],2,"NTFS combines group permissions, but an explicit deny takes precedence over an allow, so the Deny Write blocks saving. The idea that share permissions override NTFS is tempting, but the most restrictive of share and NTFS applies, and no share restriction was mentioned.","Domain 4: Permission problems",["The most restrictive of share and NTFS applies, and no restrictive share permission is mentioned.","Group membership order does not matter; NTFS evaluates all applicable entries together.",null,"Modify includes write and create, so it would allow saving without the deny."],3],["x60",0,4,"Logs show a service account signing in interactively at 3 AM from a workstation it never uses. What should the admin do first?",["Disable the account or reset its credentials, then investigate the source","Wait a week to gather more evidence before acting","Rebuild every server in the environment from scratch","Clear the security logs so they do not fill up"],0,"Containment comes first: disabling or resetting the credentials stops further misuse while the source is investigated. Waiting for more evidence is tempting, but it gives an attacker more time with a working account.","Domain 4: Compromised accounts",[null,"Waiting gives an attacker more time with a working account and risks more damage.","Rebuilding every server is drastic before scoping the incident; containment of the account comes first.","Clearing logs destroys evidence needed to investigate the incident."],2]]);
